entry x-ray
term: X-ray
image: ../../assets/images/terms/X-ray.webp
related_terms: gamma-ray
related_terms: ionizing-radiation
related_terms: linear-accelerator
related_terms: roentgen
quick_facts: Discovered by Röntgen (1895) — first Nobel in Physics
quick_facts: Born from electron interactions, unlike nuclear gamma rays
quick_facts: Softer than most gammas but the ranges overlap
quick_facts: CT scans stack hundreds of X-ray slices
quick_facts: Lead aprons attenuate scattered rays during imaging

sources:
    {"organization":"Nobel Prize","title":"Wilhelm Röntgen Biography","url":"https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/physics/1901/rontgen/biographical/"}

definition:
    Electromagnetic radiation born when fast electrons slam into matter — decelerating violently (bremsstrahlung) or knocking inner-shell electrons loose. Wilhelm Röntgen discovered them accidentally in 1895 and photographed his wife's hand within weeks, launching medical imaging and winning the first Nobel Prize in Physics (1901). Energies overlap with gamma rays; the difference is origin — X-rays come from electron processes, gammas from nuclei. Hospitals fire LINAC-built beams through patients millions of times daily.

debunking_snippet:
    “X-rays make you radioactive afterward.” Impossible — X-rays pass through and vanish at light speed. Only neutron bombardment activates materials, and hospital X-rays aren't neutrons.
